      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Discoveries in Optics</strong><br>\nNewton also conducted experiments on light and optics during his time away from Cambridge. He discovered that white light is composed of a spectrum of colors, which can be separated by a prism. Newton's work on light challenged the prevailing theories of his time, which believed that colors were a mixture of light and darkness.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Principia</strong><br>\nIn 1687, Newton published his most famous work, \"Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica,\" commonly known as the Principia. In this book, he laid out the three laws of motion, which form the basis of classical mechanics, and the law of universal gravitation. The Principia is considered one of the most important works in the history of science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Newton's Later Years and Knighthood</strong><br>\nAfter publishing the Principia, Newton received many accolades and was appointed as the Lucasian Professor of Mathematics at Cambridge. He moved to London in 1696 to work as a warden, and later master, of the Royal Mint. In recognition of his service to the country and his scientific achievements, Newton was knighted by Queen Anne in 1705.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
