          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Newton and Alchemy</strong><br>\nDespite his scientific genius, Newton had a lifelong interest in alchemy. He wrote more on this subject than on mathematics and science combined. Alchemy was a blend of science, philosophy, and mysticism that sought to transform base metals into precious ones, and Newton devoted much time to the study of these ancient traditions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious Views and Prophecies</strong><br>\nNewton was a deeply religious person and had a particular interest in the literal interpretation of the Bible. He spent considerable time studying biblical texts and believed that knowledge of the natural world could reveal divine truths. Newton also made predictions about the end of the world based on his study of the Bible, which garnered attention and controversy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Isaac Newton</strong><br>\nNewton died on March 31, 1727, and was buried in Westminster Abbey. He never married or had children, dedicating his life to science and knowledge. His discoveries laid the groundwork for centuries of scientific advancement, and his legacy as one of the greatest scientists of all time endures. Newton's insights into light, motion, and gravity have shaped our understanding of the universe.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
